# Git — implement first, PR only on request

The user owns `main`. The agent never lands work there.

## Default (every prompt)

Implement the change. Leave it **uncommitted**.

Do **not** `git commit`, `git push`, or `gh pr create` until the user explicitly asks. Asking for a PR means open a PR — never merge.

## When they ask to commit

Commit (Conventional Commits, English) **and push immediately**. Do not wait for a second push request. Do not open a PR unless they also asked for one.

If the branch already has an open PR, edit that PR’s title and body so they match **every** commit and file on the branch (not only the latest).

## When they ask for a PR

1. Branch off current `main` (`feat/…` / `fix/…`) if not already on one. Do not commit on `main`.
2. One concern per branch/PR. Split unrelated requests. Stack later PRs when they depend on an earlier branch.
3. Commit (Conventional Commits, English). Push. Open a PR whose body covers **all** commits in the PR.
4. Required checks: `quality`, `api-test`, `web-e2e`. Fix the PR if CI is red. Do not merge.
5. Stop. Tell the user the PR URL. They review and merge.

Later commits on that PR: push, then edit the PR description so it stays consistent.

## Never

- Commit / push / open a PR unprompted
- `git push` to `main` / `master`
- `gh pr merge`, merge-button equivalents, or “merge when green” loops
- Force-push to `main`

## After they merge

GitHub **deletes the PR head branch** (`delete_branch_on_merge`). Do not recreate it. The merge commit and the PR keep the history.

Do not fast-forward local `main` or restack later PRs unless they ask. Local leftover: `git fetch --prune` and delete the local branch if it still exists.

After `git pull` / branch switch: `npm run build:packages` (`dist/` is gitignored; stale types kill the API watch).
